{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfboairxFUm31vpjekNVf", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "You can, but only with `--dry-run`, and check `EXPORT_BUCKET` before you run it.\n\n**Why:** a project memory (a hazard note written by a person on the team) says that in staging, `EXPORT_BUCKET` still points at the **production** bucket. It's left over from the 2025 migration. `scripts/export.py` writes to whatever bucket that variable names (`scripts/export.py:10`), so a real run against staging would overwrite the prod monthly invoice reports with staging data.\n\n**Safe way to test:**\n```bash\n# In the staging environment, check where it would write first\necho \"$EXPORT_BUCKET\"\n\npython scripts/export.py --env staging --dry-run\n```\nThe script prints the target bucket on startup (`exporting staging to <bucket> (dry_run=True)`), so you can confirm the destination from the output too.\n\n**Notes:**\n- The `export.py` in the repo is currently just a stub. It parses the arguments and prints a line, without writing anything yet, so today a run wouldn't do damage. If the version deployed to staging is the full one, or the stub gets filled in, the hazard is real. Use `--dry-run` either way.\n- I couldn't check the staging environment config from this repo, so the claim that `EXPORT_BUCKET` points at prod comes from the memory, not from anything I confirmed. If `echo $EXPORT_BUCKET` in staging shows a staging bucket, the misconfiguration may have been fixed. Let me know and I'll update the memory.\n- For a full non-dry-run test, override the bucket explicitly so it can't fall through to prod. For example: `EXPORT_BUCKET=<a-scratch-bucket> python scripts/export.py --env staging`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 371, "cache_read_input_tokens": 24841, "cache_creation": {"ephemer